WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.208
- Really, guys?

00:00:19.208 --> 00:00:22.041
(whimsical music)

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:24.453
- I'm Donal Skehan.

00:00:24.453 --> 00:00:26.410
I've been cooking since
about the age of 10.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:27.850
I grew up in Dublin, Ireland,

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:31.003
and today, we're gonna
try some Irish treats.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.453
Lovely.

00:00:35.453 --> 00:00:37.390
We've got potatoes, carrots, cabbage.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.270
Got a bit of mustard.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.180
This is looking strong.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.800
Traditionally, for St. Patrick's Day,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:41.970
we have bacon and cabbage,

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.490
but when you understand the origins

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.190
of what corned beef and
cabbage is all about,

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:46.480
it is an Irish dish.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:47.340
This looks pretty good.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.173
Let's try some.

00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:49.006
Do we have a knife?

00:00:49.006 --> 00:00:50.810
I've been given a fairly extreme knife.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:51.773
It's tender, right?

00:00:53.237 --> 00:00:54.077
Mm.

00:00:54.077 --> 00:00:55.010
Whoo.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.670
What sort of mustard is that?

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:57.940
That is some hot mustard.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:58.773
It tastes good.

00:00:58.773 --> 00:00:59.760
It's got that salty hit.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:00.790
I'm not opposed to a bit

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.376
of corned beef and cabbage in my life.

00:01:02.376 --> 00:01:05.223
(upbeat music)

00:01:05.223 --> 00:01:06.056
Nice.

00:01:06.056 --> 00:01:08.500
You cannot go wrong with
a plate of fish and chips.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.500
As an Irish nation,

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.900
we eat a lot of fish and chips,

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.290
but I guess so do the
people in the UK, as well.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.380
Nice chips on the side.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.420
Tartar sauce comes in
this little plastic thing.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.020
I grew in Howth, in Dublin,

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.020
which is a beautiful
little fishing village,

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:21.640
so I have high expectations.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.387
Well, let's check out how the
Americans do fish and chips.

00:01:24.387 --> 00:01:26.491
(chuckling)

00:01:26.491 --> 00:01:27.324
Mm.

00:01:27.324 --> 00:01:28.157
Not bad.

00:01:28.157 --> 00:01:29.270
Apart from the soggy batter.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.250
I could live with that, for the moment,

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.090
but the flavor is there.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.050
It's been well-seasoned.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.570
Go onto the chips, or fries,

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.730
as you call them in America.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:37.563
Not bad.

00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:38.396
Not great.

00:01:38.396 --> 00:01:39.510
As an American version goes,

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:40.410
I'm not sure it's quite there.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:41.500
I think you need to come to Ireland

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.830
to try a proper fish and chips.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.760
(upbeat music)

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.720
I'm worried about what this could even be.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.150
Oh dear God.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.450
I think Americans just know

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.010
how to use the color green.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.380
Is it meant to be this thick?

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.380
Where did you even get green pancakes?

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.010
Lonking food coloring into something

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.010
and calling it Irish?

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:59.550
The Irish part of me is
still somewhat skeptical.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.590
I think it's time to try some.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.270
Oh, there's layers.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:07.083
Lucky Charms, and what looks
like some sort of green pea.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.440
Chewy, sweet, marshmallowy.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:11.320
Why is it so thick?

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.903
Should there be grains in here?

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:14.703
Wow.

00:02:14.703 --> 00:02:15.536
That's sugary.

00:02:15.536 --> 00:02:16.369
I have no idea what's in here.

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.130
It kinda tastes like cereal
milkshake that's green.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:20.920
Nothing necessarily traditional Irish.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.370
You could throw in a bit of
whiskey and I'd be happy.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.500
Never in my entire Irish life

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.970
have I had my tongue green.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.810
That is the influence of America.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.018
No less.

00:02:30.018 --> 00:02:32.160
(upbeat music)

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.630
Ah, potato.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.170
Probably the most stereotypical thing,

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.180
when it comes to Irish food,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.710
we've a long, long history of potatoes.

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:40.660
There's lots of great
ways to cook potatoes.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.840
This particular dish has
not celebrated any of those.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.660
There's some kind of stringy hairs in it.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.030
With little lumps in them.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.711
I don't think that's
ever part of the dish,

00:02:49.711 --> 00:02:51.116
that I ever had.

00:02:51.116 --> 00:02:52.870
Good attempt, I think?

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.300
We have moved on from just potatoes,

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.820
but actually, when it's a rainy day,

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.630
and I just want serious comfort,

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.410
it does come back to a nice mashed potato,

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:02.840
a bit of Irish stew,

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.510
lovely crispy potatoes on top.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:05.343
I love potatoes.

00:03:05.343 --> 00:03:06.176
Don't you?

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.960
Lucky Charms,

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.620
the most stereotypical Irish thing

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:12.840
you could possibly buy.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.890
There's rainbows, pots of gold,

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:16.210
don't know what that is,

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:17.810
little peach or cherry,

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.350
and there's definitely
none of those in Ireland.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.740
This is a bowl of sunshine right there,

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.220
and a whole host of sugar, as well.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.200
You can't buy them in Ireland.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.710
If you ever saw Lucky Charms as a kid,

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:28.140
you were super excited.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.290
You were drawn in by the sugar,

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.860
and drawn in by the neon colors.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:32.693
You know what?

00:03:32.693 --> 00:03:33.526
It's a bowl of sugar.

00:03:33.526 --> 00:03:34.400
This is why it's appealing,

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.030
and I kinda like the little marshmallows.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.330
As much as this is the most
American version of Irish food,

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:40.312
I'm not opposed.

00:03:40.312 --> 00:03:41.350
Mm-hmm.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:42.443
Hmm, mm, mm.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.070
Ireland has the most incredible
food scene at the moment,

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:47.370
really great food producers,

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.570
amazing restaurants.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:52.270
We're a small nation
that has one day a year

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:53.800
that is celebrated by the world.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.360
If the Americans decide they
wanna paint everything green,

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:57.193
fill it with sugar,

00:03:57.193 --> 00:03:59.360
and turn every milkshake
in the land green,

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.530
I am on board.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:01.960
Once it celebrates Ireland,

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.160
and spreads the word of how
bloody brilliant we are,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.430
I'm a happy man.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.620
I think I might just go off into a corner,

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:09.970
and eat these by myself.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:10.971
Happy St. Patrick's Day.

00:04:10.971 --> 00:04:13.804
(whimsical music)

